{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01XkJsPUy6U5zVeicvfiGuNL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the `colourblind` C library (c_src/src/lib.c, c_src/include/lib.h).\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Public ABI, as reported by `nm -D` on the C shared object: a single exported\n4\t//! function, `colourblind`. The three per-impairment transform helpers are\n5\t//! `static` in the C source and therefore intentionally NOT exported here.\n6\t//!\n7\t//! # Behaviour preserved exactly from the C\n8\t//!\n9\t//! * Each helper reads all three components into locals *before* writing any of\n10\t//!   them back, then stores in the order Red, Green, Blue. This is observable\n11\t//!   when the caller passes aliasing pointers, so raw pointer reads/writes in\n12\t//!   that exact order are used rather than a safe by-value abstraction.\n13\t//! * The `switch` in `colourblind` has no `default` arm, so an impairment value\n14\t//!   outside {0, 1, 2} is a silent no-op. The C compiler compares the enum as an\n15\t//!   unsigned int, so out-of-range and \"negative\" values are no-ops too.\n16\t//! * No NULL checks: the C dereferences unconditionally, so this does too.\n17\t//! * All arithmetic is IEEE-754 single precision evaluated left-to-right, with\n18\t//!   no fused multiply-add contraction and no promotion to double.\n19\t//!\n20\t//! # Why the arithmetic goes through `sse`\n21\t//!\n22\t//! For finite inputs, plain `f32` operators already reproduce the C bit-for-bit.\n23\t//! They differ only in the *sign of a NaN result* when two NaN operands with\n24\t//! different signs meet in one expression: x86's `ADDSS`/`SUBSS`/`MULSS` return\n25\t//! the **destination** operand in that case, so which NaN survives depends on\n26\t//! the operand order the compiler happened to pick. LLVM canonicalises those\n27\t//! operands differently from GCC (and rewrites `a - c*b` into an add of a\n28\t//! negated constant), which flips some NaN sign bit